# Built a dedicated spaced-repetition workspace and native Anki portability for Keating

Rollout context: In `/home/diogenes/Projects/keating`, the user wanted the Coming Up experience substantially improved for spaced repetition, learner-controlled priorities, and Anki interoperability. The user specified a dedicated dynamic Kanban-like interface with draggable items and delegated priority/import format decisions.

## Task 1: Coming Up spaced-repetition Kanban

Outcome: success

Preference signals:
- The user explicitly requested that “coming up should be a dedicated and dynamic kanban like interface with dragable items” -> future feature work should favor a dedicated task workspace with direct manipulation rather than a static analytics panel.
- The user delegated secondary decisions (“figure the rest”, “your choice”) -> when scope is clear, choose sensible defaults and preserve the core requested interaction instead of asking unnecessary follow-ups.

Key steps:
- Located the existing static `ComingUpPanel` in `web/src/components/UsageCharts.tsx`, existing SM-2 flashcard state in `web/src/keating/srs.ts`, deck persistence in `web/src/keating/storage.ts`, and existing navigation/routing patterns.
- Added a dedicated `/coming-up` route and `web/src/pages/ComingUp.tsx` with draggable Focus, Maintain, and Low priority lanes.
- Added priority-aware queue derivation in `web/src/keating/coming-up.ts`, persistence of explicit priorities, dynamic inclusion of due cards/open checks/needs-review topics, and protection against hiding overdue work when moved to low priority.
- Added inline review actions, live due counts, keyboard/touch select controls as a non-drag alternative, and clock refresh on focus/interval.
- Removed the old static Coming Up panel from Usage charts and wired navigation/model session context.

Reusable knowledge:
- Keating already had a tested SM-2 variant: ratings Again/Hard/Good/Easy, due timestamps, lapses, and deck-level scheduling helpers.
- The implementation intentionally separates learner intent priority from algorithmic due dates: dragging changes priority/lane, not SRS scheduling; overdue evidence remains visible.
- Browser verification covered `/coming-up`, drag persistence, keyboard/touch priority changes, review grading reducing due count from 6 to 5, desktop light/dark layouts, and 390px mobile layout.

Failures and how to do differently:
- The repository lacked a project-local Impeccable bundle; the first project-relative `context.mjs` call failed with `MODULE_NOT_FOUND`, then the installed user-level bundle was used.
- `vet` could not execute because neither `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` nor `ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN` was set. Treat vet as unverified in this environment and rely on tests/build/typecheck unless credentials are available.

References:
- `web/src/pages/ComingUp.tsx`
- `web/src/keating/coming-up.ts`
- `web/src/keating/srs.ts`
- `web/src/keating/storage.ts`
- `web/src/components/UsageCharts.tsx`
- `web/src/test/coming-up.test.ts`
- `617 pass, 0 fail` from `bun test`

## Task 2: Native Anki import/export

Outcome: success

Preference signals:
- The user said native Anki support was “ideally easy for people to move anki stuff in and out” -> prioritize native `.apkg` round trips while retaining a transparent text fallback.

Key steps:
- Added `web/src/keating/anki-package.ts` using `sql.js`, `fzstd`, and ZIP handling.
- Supports `collection.anki21b`, `collection.anki21`, and `collection.anki2`; also CSV import and TSV fallback export.
- Converts Anki HTML/cloze content to inert safe review text, intentionally excludes media and executable/template styling, and preserves scheduling/tags.
- Preserved source deck IDs, card IDs, note IDs, and GUIDs so repeated export/import remains update-friendly; merging uses newer local/imported timestamps without overwriting newer review state.
- Added `web/src/test/anki-package.test.ts` covering APKG round trips, cloze/HTML conversion, CSV quoting, TSV fallback, identity preservation, and repeat-import merging.

Reusable knowledge:
- Native APKG round-trip tests passed, including original Anki identities and scheduling.
- Browser export produced `/tmp/keating-browser-export.apkg` with status `Exported 6 cards as a native Anki package.`
- Build added `sql.js`, `@types/sql.js`, and `fzstd` to `web/package.json` and lockfiles.

Failures and how to do differently:
- Initial export compatibility review identified that generated IDs could cause duplicate material in Anki. The durable fix was optional Anki source metadata on decks/cards and stable ID generation for non-imported cards.

References:
- `web/src/keating/anki-package.ts`
- `web/src/test/anki-package.test.ts`
- `web/package.json`
- `web/bun.lock`
- Anki package guidance referenced: `https://docs.ankiweb.net/importing/packaged-decks.html`

## Task 3: Validation and integration

Outcome: success

Key steps:
- Ran focused tests: `14 pass, 0 fail` across Anki, Coming Up, and session-start hooks.
- Ran full web suite: `617 pass, 0 fail`, 9,609 expect calls.
- Ran `bun run typecheck`: passed.
- Ran `bun run build`: Panda codegen, Vite production build, Nitro server build, and PWA generation all passed.
- Ran `git diff --check`: passed.

Reusable knowledge:
- Full build emits non-fatal existing warnings about externalized Node modules and unresolved KaTeX font paths; the build still exits successfully.
- The worktree contained substantial pre-existing unrelated changes. Future agents should preserve those and avoid interpreting the entire dirty tree as belonging to this feature.

## Reusable knowledge

- Use one `site.standard.publication` and `site.standard.document` per article with `at.markpub.markdown`; retain `textContent` as plaintext fallback. Reader/parser code is `web/src/keating/standard-site.ts`, `web/server/utils/atproto-blog.ts`, and `web/src/pages/AtprotoBlog.tsx`; routes include `/api/blog`, `/blog`, `/blog/**`, and `/.well-known/site.standard.publication`. [Task 1]
- Resolve a configured handle/DID through its DID document and permit `KEATING_BLOG_PDS_URL` as a local/staged Tranquil override. The migration script converts 46 legacy posts to 47 records in dry-run; inject `KEATING_BLOG_PDS_URL`, `KEATING_BLOG_IDENTIFIER`, and `KEATING_BLOG_APP_PASSWORD` through secret store, never shell arguments/history. [Task 1]
- Focused publisher/parser/server tests (11), typecheck, and Vite/Nitro build passed. A Chrome pass rendered `/blog` cleanly. [Task 1]

## Failures and how to do differently

- A shared feed request must not be cancelled merely because one page unmounts; avoid aborting it and guard only state updates with an `active` flag to prevent cross-route races. If Playwriter relay times out, isolated browser sessions were more reliable. [Task 1]
- Live Tranquil write was intentionally not run because account/credentials were absent, and Vet was unavailable/stalled; do not claim PDS publication or Vet review. [Task 1]

